            <div class="asm-section-header">
                <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
                <p>
                    Before purchasing a calculator, many students, parents, teachers,
                    and professionals ask similar questions. Below are the most common
                    calculator buying questions along with detailed answers to help you
                    choose the right model for your needs.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="asm-faq">

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>1. Which calculator is best for high school students?</h3>
                    <p>
                        It depends on your school's requirements. If your classes mainly
                        cover algebra, geometry, trigonometry, and general mathematics,
                        the <strong>Sharp EL-W516TBSL</strong> or
                        <strong>Rudnia Scientific Calculator</strong> are excellent
                        choices. If your school requires graphing functions for
                        Pre-Calculus or Calculus, choose the
                        <strong>TI-84 Plus CE</strong> or
                        <strong>TI-84 Plus CE Python Edition</strong>.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>2. Do I really need a graphing calculator?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Not always. Students taking basic mathematics usually need only a
                        scientific calculator. Graphing calculators become valuable for
                        Calculus, Statistics, Engineering, Physics, Computer Science,
                        Advanced Mathematics, and many university STEM courses.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>3. What is the difference between a scientific calculator and a graphing calculator?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Scientific calculators solve mathematical equations but do not
                        display graphs. Graphing calculators perform scientific
                        calculations while also plotting equations, displaying graphs,
                        solving systems of equations, working with matrices, statistics,
                        vectors, regression analysis, and many advanced mathematical
                        operations.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>4. Which calculator is best for engineering students?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Engineering students usually benefit from graphing calculators.
                        The TI-84 Plus CE, TI-84 Python Edition, Nadex Graphing
                        Calculator, and iPepul Graphing Calculator all provide features
                        useful for engineering mathematics, calculus, matrices,
                        statistics, and graph visualization.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>5. Which calculator should finance students buy?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Finance, accounting, MBA, CFA, banking, investment, and business
                        students should purchase the
                        <strong>Texas Instruments BA II Plus Professional</strong>.
                        It supports Time Value of Money (TVM), Cash Flow Analysis,
                        Net Present Value (NPV), Internal Rate of Return (IRR),
                        bond calculations, depreciation, and loan amortization.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>6. Which calculator offers the best value for money?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Buyers looking for outstanding value should consider the
                        Rudnia Scientific Calculator,
                        Sharp EL-W516TBSL,
                        Victor 1190 Executive,
                        DECKLIT Mechanical Calculator,
                        or iPepul Graphing Calculator.
                        These products deliver excellent functionality relative to their
                        price.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>7. Is Casio better than Texas Instruments?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Neither brand is universally better—they simply serve different
                        needs. Casio offers excellent affordability, dependable quality,
                        and beginner-friendly scientific calculators. Texas Instruments
                        dominates many high school and college classrooms because many
                        teachers design coursework specifically around the TI-84
                        platform.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>8. Are graphing calculators worth the extra cost?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Yes—if your coursework requires graphing. A graphing calculator
                        can remain useful for several years and often supports students
                        from high school through university. If you only need everyday
                        arithmetic or basic algebra, however, a scientific calculator is
                        usually the better investment.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>9. What is the best calculator under $20?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Some of the best calculators under $20 featured in this guide
                        include:
                    </p>

                    <ul>
                        <li>Sharp EL233SB</li>
                        <li>Casio SL-310SV</li>
                        <li>Victor 1190 Executive</li>
                        <li>DECKLIT Mechanical Calculator</li>
                        <li>Rudnia Scientific Calculator (often available near this range during promotions)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>10. Which calculator is easiest for beginners?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Beginners generally find the Sharp EL233SB, Casio SL-310SV,
                        and Casio fx-55PLUS the easiest to learn because they provide
                        straightforward interfaces without overwhelming users with
                        advanced engineering menus.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>11. Should I buy a solar calculator?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Yes. Solar-powered calculators with battery backup generally
                        provide longer operating life and require fewer battery
                        replacements. They are especially useful for students and office
                        workers who use calculators daily.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="asm-faq-item">
                    <h3>12. Can I use a calculator on standardized exams?</h3>
                    <p>
                        Every examination has different rules. Always verify your exam's
                        approved calculator list before purchasing. Some exams prohibit
                        programmable calculators, while others specifically allow or
                        recommend certain graphing calculator models.
                    </p>
                </div>

